{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00153 - MONDO_1567",
  "verbalization": "Ergocalciferol, a form of vitamin D, is implicated in the pathogenesis of nephrocalcinosis through a series of interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Ergocalciferol is associated with the development of nephrocalcinosis via the intermediate metabolite dihydrotachysterol. Both osteomalacia and rickets, which are conditions associated with vitamin D deficiency, are linked to dihydrotachysterol, and this metabolite is known to contribute to calcium deposition in the kidneys, a hallmark of nephrocalcinosis. The presence of a direct indication from Ergocalciferol to dihydrotachysterol, along with the established role of dihydrotachysterol in kidney calcium deposition, supports a mechanistic link between Ergocalciferol and nephrocalcinosis. The scores from the paths indicate a strong, consistent relationship, reinforcing the hypothesis that Ergocalciferol may contribute to the development of nephrocalcinosis through its role in the metabolic pathway involving dihydrotachysterol.",
